bent advantage in a D+2 district amid a favorable national environment"], "rationale": "Gillen combines incumbency, a roughly twenty-to-one fundraising advantage, and a ten-rater consensus at Likely D, while markets price her win odds near 90 percent. With no public general-election polling, the margin rests on those fundamentals, pointing to a clear Democratic edge that stops short of safe.", "watch": "A late wave of outside spending against Gillen or the first public polls showing Driscoll within single digits would move the race toward tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Laura Gillen (D) is running."]}, "overview": "NY-04 pairs first-term incumbent Laura Gillen, a Democrat, with Republican Jeanine Driscoll in a Long Island district that leans Democratic by about two points. Gillen has dominated the money race, raising roughly $5.1 million to Driscoll's $265,000, and enters the fall with nearly $3.8 million on hand. No general-election polls are available, so the picture rests on fundamentals and outside assessments.\n\nExpert raters span Tilt D to Safe D, with the consensus at Likely D, and prediction markets put Democratic chances near 90 percent. Gillen has spent the summer highlighting local work on policing and Long Island water funding, while Driscoll won a contested Republican primary in June. The combination of incumbency, financial advantage, and a favorable national environment points to a clear Democratic edge, though the district's swing history keeps the race from reaching safe territory.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9622, "margin": 11.83}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9401, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9422, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9272, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9344, "margin": 11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9323, "margin": 11.0